<p class="lead">Most process serving companies start on email, spreadsheets and a phone. It works until the volume grows. Then jobs get missed, servers chase the office for details, and <a href="/blogs/automate-affidavit-of-service">affidavits</a> take longer than the serve itself. Here is what good process server software needs to do, and how to decide between buying a product and building your own.</p>
<h2 id="the-job-from-request-to-invoice">The job, from request to invoice</h2>
<p>Every serve follows the same path:</p>
<ol>
  <li>A law firm sends a request, usually by email with PDFs attached.</li>
  <li>The office enters the job and assigns a server.</li>
  <li>The server makes attempts and records each one.</li>
  <li>The serve is completed, or the job is closed as non-service.</li>
  <li>An affidavit is prepared, signed and sent to the client.</li>
  <li>The client is invoiced.</li>
</ol>
<p>Process server software should handle all six steps in one place, so nobody retypes the same details three times.</p>
<h2 id="features-that-matter">Features that matter</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Intake from email and PDFs: the system reads the request and fills in the job: names, addresses, documents, deadlines and the client's reference.</li>
  <li>A verification screen: office staff check what was read, with the original document beside the form, before the job goes live.</li>
  <li>Mobile dispatch: servers see their jobs on a phone, with routes and notes.</li>
  <li>Attempt logging: each attempt is saved with GPS location, time and photos.</li>
  <li>Affidavits: generated from the job data and the attempt log, then signed electronically.</li>
  <li>Invoicing: created when the job closes, using each client's own rates.</li>
  <li>A client portal: law firms see status without calling you.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="signs-youve-outgrown-spreadsheets">Signs you've outgrown spreadsheets</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Staff spend more time typing requests than managing servers.</li>
  <li>Clients call to ask "has this been served yet?"</li>
  <li>Your scripts or formulas only one person understands.</li>
  <li>Affidavits and invoices go out days after the serve.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="buy-or-build">Buy or build?</h2>
<p>A standard product works when your process is standard. Building your own makes sense when you receive requests from many firms in different formats, charge each client differently, work in several jurisdictions with different affidavit formats, or want to offer the platform to other serving companies.</p>
<p>We rebuilt a <a href="/case-studies/flss-legal-process-serving">process serving company</a>'s operations that ran on Gmail, spreadsheets and a 3,500-line script. The new platform reads requests from emails and PDFs, dispatches servers with GPS and photo logs, generates affidavits and creates invoices automatically. See our <a href="/solutions/legal">legal page</a> for how we approach these builds.</p>
